Based on my research, here's what you need to know about upgrading from MSTest 3.8.0 to the latest (4.2.3):

## Breaking Changes for Your Project

### 1. `[ExpectedException]` is removed in MSTest 4.x
This is the biggest impact on your code. Replace with `Assert.ThrowsExactly<T>()`:

```csharp
// Before (3.x)
[ExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentNullException))]
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_NullInput_ThrowsArgumentNull()
{
    Validate(null);
}

// After (4.x)
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_NullInput_ThrowsArgumentNull()
{
    Assert.ThrowsExactly<ArgumentNullException>(() => Validate(null));
}
```

### 2. Analyzer MSTEST0032 (warning → error in 4.x)
Your `Assert.IsTrue(true)` will produce a warning/error for always-true conditions.

### 3. Analyzer MSTEST0001 elevated to warning
You'll get a warning to explicitly set `[assembly: Parallelize]` or `[assembly: DoNotParallelize]`.

### 4. .NET 8.0+ required
Your `net8.0` TFM is fine — no change needed.

### 5. New test runner (MTP) is the default
MSTest 4.x uses the Microsoft Testing Platform runner by default instead of VSTest. Your `dotnet test` CI commands may need updating, or set `<UseVSTest>true</UseVSTest>` to keep the old behavior.

